globalThis.DanceLibrarySummaries = globalThis.DanceLibrarySummaries || {};
globalThis.DanceLibrarySummaries["isabelle-felicien-beginner-1162e046"] = {
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/01 - Basic 3 Steps 5 Steps Slow Motion and Rotation.mp4": "- **[00:18]** - **Basic Step Spatial Awareness & Weight Transfer**: We initiate the fundamental side basic by shifting off the center axis. The leader drives with the left foot and the follower with the right, collecting the feet into a neutral position where the center of gravity returns precisely to a 50/50 bipedal distribution. \n- **[01:41]** - **Biomechanics of the Kizomba Walk**: Observe the flat-foot articulation required for a grounded Kizomba walk. You must avoid plantarflexion (stepping on the toes); instead, apply full body weight into the floor with each step, shifting 100% of your mass over the new supporting leg to establish a solid axis before the next movement.\n- **[02:26]** - **The 3-Step & The \"Marca\"**: We transition into a 3-step sequence culminating in a \"Marca.\" The Marca requires high core control to tap the foot without committing mass, acting as a structural reset. You must bring your center of gravity back to a perfect midline, stabilizing the pelvis before initiating the next phrase.\n- **[04:19]** - **Linear 3-Step Spatial Awareness**: Applying the 3-step in a linear slot demands strict spatial management. The leader must drive forward using the core to displace the follower backward, maintaining an equal distance between the bodies. The Marca acts as a braking mechanism to arrest forward momentum seamlessly.\n- **[05:06]** - **Step Stride & Leader/Follower Communication**: Your stride length dictates the clarity of your lead. Steps must be at least the length of one foot; anything smaller dampens the biomechanical signal transmitted to your partner. A confident, grounded stride ensures the follower can read your center of gravity shifts without ambiguity.\n- **[05:32]** - **5-Step Sequence Weight Management**: Extending to a 5-step pattern tests your continuous weight transfer endurance. Notice that the Marca consistently lands on the leader's left foot and the follower's right foot. This absolute predictability in weight distribution is what anchors the partnership's synchronization.\n- **[06:41]** - **Slow Motion Mechanics & Core Isolation**: The slow-motion step is a masterclass in kinetic control, halving the tempo (two beats per step). Do not artificially pause or separate the foot placement from the weight shift. Instead, utilize deep core engagement to create a continuous, viscous transfer of mass from one axis to the other.\n- **[08:31]** - **Auditory Processing & Initiation Mechanics**: Unlike Salsa's strict 8-count grid, Kizomba relies on intuitive pulse recognition. You initiate movement based on the heavy downbeat, utilizing your core to stabilize and prepare the frame before leading the follower into the first weight transfer.\n- **[11:11]** - **Solo 5-Step Axis Drilling**: During solo practice of the 5-step, focus aggressively on your vertical alignment. Your shoulders must remain stacked over your hips, preventing any anterior or posterior pitching. This solo isolation builds the stability required for proper partner connection.\n- **[12:31]** - **Sustained Kinetic Chain in Slow Motion**: When dancing slow-motion to music, your kinetic chain must remain fluid. Engage your glutes and lower abdominals to act as a decelerator, ensuring the foot glides into the floor while the body weight follows in a perfectly synchronized, sustained glide.\n- **[16:11]** - **Quarter and Half Turn Rotational Mechanics**: Introducing torque for the quarter and half turns requires stepping in an arc, not just opening the hip. The leader must drive forward and across the follower's line to create rotational momentum, utilizing the core to twist the frame as a single cohesive block without breaking the slot.\n- **[19:11]** - **Frame Integrity & Partner Connection**: Establishing the closed Kizomba frame. The leader's right arm connects deeply across the follower's latissimus dorsi, while the feet interlock to offset the center lines. Maintain upright posture—do not lean into your partner—relying entirely on frame compression and engaged lats to transmit kinetic energy.\n- **[20:41]** - **Integrated Partner Mechanics**: Combining all elements in closed hold tests your frame's resistance and responsiveness. The leader initiates all linear and rotational displacement from the core, not the arms, while the follower meets this energy with an active, toned frame to ensure instantaneous weight transfer mirroring.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/02 - Woman and Man Saida.mp4": "- **[01:10]** - **Spatial Awareness & Axis Shifts**: The instructors demonstrate the structural path of the Woman's Saida. Observe how the leader's line of dance is maintained straight backward, while the follower is directed off the slot to the side and then back into the original trajectory. This requires precise spatial awareness to manage the shifting axis without allowing your center of gravity to compromise your partner's balance.\n- **[01:22]** - **Weight Transfer in Woman Saida**: Detailed breakdown of the foot articulation. The leader initiates with a forward weight transfer on the left foot, creating compression, before shifting momentum backward for the next three steps. The follower receives this energy, stepping backward on the right foot, then articulating a diagonal forward step on the left to clear the leader's path, maintaining continuous grounding through the floor.\n- **[03:45]** - **Core Engagement & Dissociation**: The leader introduces chest isolations as the primary mechanism for directing the follower. You must dissociate the upper body (thoracic spine) from the lower body (pelvis/hips). The hips remain square to the line of dance while the chest rotates to alter the follower's trajectory, relying entirely on core engagement to maintain stability and prevent the hips from swiveling.\n- **[05:32]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in Closed Frame**: Transitioning the chest isolation into partner connection. The leader rotates the ribcage to the right on count two, which organically redirects the follower's forward momentum into an outward diagonal step. The follower must maintain frame integrity, allowing their own chest to remain perfectly parallel with the leader's rotating sternum without anticipating the movement or breaking connection.\n- **[07:15]** - **Frame Integrity & Distance Management**: To troubleshoot pulling or pushing with the arms, the instructors create a slight gap in the closed hold. Keep your lats engaged and your arms structurally fixed; the rotational torque must originate exclusively from the core and chest. This prevents the degradation of frame integrity and ensures the follower is guided by core displacement rather than arm manipulation.\n- **[08:05]** - **Weight Transfer & Continuous Flow**: Applying the Woman Saida to music. Notice how the steps are not disjointed; there is a continuous grounding through the feet. The weight transfer must roll smoothly from toe to heel for backward steps, or heel to toe for forward steps, to maintain the signature Kizomba flow, ensuring the axis shifts seamlessly between partners without jarring vertical bounce.\n- **[10:10]** - **Spatial Awareness in Man Saida**: The spatial dynamic reverses for the Man Saida. Here, the follower maintains a strictly linear backward trajectory while the leader displaces their own axis diagonally outside the slot. The leader must meticulously manage the distance to ensure they clear the follower's path while maintaining continuous contact and frame resistance.\n- **[11:35]** - **Core Engagement & Reverse Torque**: In the Man Saida, the leader must apply a counter-rotation with their chest. As the leader's lower body travels outside the slot to the left, the thoracic spine must rotate to the right to keep the chest parallel to the follower. This requires intense core stabilization to prevent the follower from being inadvertently pulled off their linear path by the leader's lateral momentum.\n- **[12:45]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in Man Saida**: Executing the movement in closed hold requires ultimate trust in the frame. The leader's chest isolation communicates to the follower to continue stepping linearly backward. The follower must maintain a consistent backward weight transfer, matching the leader's compression, while the leader navigates the external track, managing the tension and connection points seamlessly.\n- **[13:24]** - **Frame Integrity during Axis Displacement**: Practicing the Man Saida with a slight gap highlights the importance of maintaining a solid frame while displacing the axis. The leader's arms must remain locked into their core structure to support the follower's backward momentum. Any loss of tone in the frame will result in the follower collapsing into the space created by the leader's outward step, ruining the structural geometry.\n- **[14:24]** - **Dynamic Spatial Awareness & Flow**: Alternating between basic steps, Woman Saida, and Man Saida to music requires rapid recalibration of spatial awareness and connection points. The leader must decisively initiate the torque for the Woman Saida or the counter-torque for the Man Saida, while both partners maintain a grounded, continuous weight transfer. Ensure your frame remains consistently toned to translate these micro-shifts in core rotation instantaneously.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/03 - Balanced move and Touch Steps plus Longer  Double Women Saida.mp4": "- **[00:42]** - **Frame Integrity & Natural Locomotion**: Understand that connection in Kizomba is utilized to transmit your intention through body movement rather than arm manipulation. Initiate a natural forward lean to displace your center of gravity, which will prompt an automatic reflex step to catch your balance. Apply this biomechanical principle as the foundation of walking together in a closed hold.\n- **[01:10]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in Forward/Backward Travel**: To move synchronously, initiate your movement from a neutral, upright posture, allowing your body weight to shift slightly forward. As a follower, you must immediately sense this axis displacement through the torso connection, prompting your foot to move backward to mirror the leader's balance point and maintain structural harmony.\n- **[01:34]** - **Spatial Awareness & Lateral Axis Shifts**: Apply this same principle of controlled falling to your lateral movements. Lose your balance slightly to the side, and allow your natural reflex to dictate the foot placement. Maintain a continuous, deep torso connection to ensure immediate response to these subtle lateral axis shifts without relying on visual cues.\n- **[02:02]** - **Weight Transfer & The Balanced Move Sequence**: Execute the \"Balanced Move\" through continuous weight transfers without utilizing a 'marca' (tap step). Sequence your steps by taking two initial walking steps, followed by three fluid back-and-forth directional changes, and concluding with three returning steps. Let the continuous shifting of your center of mass dictate the flow.\n- **[02:58]** - **Core Engagement & Postural Alignment**: During the Balanced Move, you must maintain an upright, natural posture without exaggerating the forward or backward lean. Engage your core to stabilize the torso, allowing your legs to articulate smoothly beneath the body. Lift your feet slightly to ensure a complete and deliberate weight transfer on every single step.\n- **[04:02]** - **Weight Transfer & The Touch Step Mechanic**: Implement the \"Touch Step\" to create a directional change where your weight transfer is intentionally arrested mid-motion. Make contact with the floor to halt your momentum, but do not fully commit your center of gravity to that leg. Leave your touch foot completely free to immediately reverse direction.\n- **[05:01]** - **Frame Integrity & Momentum Control**: Unlike a simple tap step, executing the touch step requires active resistance and precise momentum management. Allow your upper body to continue reflecting the intended direction of travel while your core and leading leg act as brakes. This generates a high-energy, responsive dynamic within your partnership.\n- **[06:19]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in Touch Steps**: Executing the touch step in a closed hold demands high responsiveness. Leaders, you must dictate the abrupt halt in weight transfer clearly through your frame. Followers, maintain tone and avoid anticipating the movement; wait for the physical cue to arrest your momentum to ensure the syncopated timing does not fail.\n- **[07:12]** - **Spatial Awareness & Musical Application**: Observe how to manage the line of dance, ensuring you have adequate space before initiating these traveling sequences. Maintain consistent body contact to provide a continuous feedback loop. This connection is crucial for executing the sharp directional changes of the touch steps accurately to the music.\n- **[10:10]** - **Spatial Awareness & The Women's Saida**: The fundamental mechanic here is displacing your follower from the primary line of dance. Leaders, step back to open the slot and guide the follower to step outside your right leg. This creates a clear path for the follower to travel linearly without obstruction.\n- **[11:24]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in the Longer Saida**: Maintain your follower on the outside track to extend the Saida indefinitely. Leaders, walk backward while guiding your follower to walk forward in the parallel slot. Keep your frame integrity solid to guide them straight down this outside line before eventually redirecting them back in front to close the pattern.\n- **[13:45]** - **Core Engagement & The Double Women's Saida**: Execute the opening of the Saida, bring the follower back in, and immediately send them out again without a concluding marca. This rapid redirection requires substantial core stability from both of you. Manage the shifting centrifugal forces carefully to maintain your shared balance during these repeated lateral displacements.\n- **[14:15]** - **Frame Integrity & Torso Rotation**: The key to leading the Double Saida is your pronounced chest rotation. To lead the follower back outside on count 5, maximize your torso rotation to the right. Utilize your frame to clearly dictate the follower's crossing step over their own center line.\n- **[16:02]** - **Weight Transfer & Complex Sequence Integration**: During your musical practice, focus on the seamless integration of continuous walking, the extended track of the Longer Saida, and the sharp rotational redirects of the Double Saida. Ensure your core mechanics of complete weight transfers and maintaining a unified axis remain constant, regardless of the pattern's complexity.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/04 - Mixt Saida  complicated Mixt Saida.mp4": "- **[00:32]** - **Weight Transfer & Spatial Awareness**: Explanation of the \"Marca\" (mark). The instructor defines it as a reset point to find optimal positioning before initiating a new pattern. This neutral stance allows both partners to re-establish their shared axis.\n- **[01:21]** - **Weight Transfer Mechanics**: To create a more fluid dance and avoid a \"staccato\" feeling, the instructors demonstrate skipping the Marca. Mechanically, this requires stabilizing weight entirely on the standing leg (leader's right, follower's left) to immediately initiate the next sequence with the free leg, eliminating the weight shift back to neutral.\n- **[01:46]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Spatial Awareness**: Breakdown of the first half of the Mixt Saida, which consists of the first four counts of the Woman's Saida. The leader guides the follower across the slot, establishing a strong frame to block her forward momentum on count four, effectively halting her progression down the line of dance.\n- **[02:10]** - **Weight Transfer & Frame Integrity**: The critical transition point between the two Saidas. Instead of marking, the leader maintains weight on the right foot and utilizes frame tension to rotate the chest to the right, guiding the follower to stay on his line while he steps out with the left foot to initiate the Men's Saida.\n- **[03:15]** - **Core Engagement & Axis Shifts**: Detailed breakdown of the transition, identifying it as a \"balance move.\" As the leader steps onto the back right foot and the follower onto the forward left foot, both must engage their core to absorb the momentum. The leader then uses this grounded compression to redirect the energy outward.\n- **[03:52]** - **Fluidity & Continuous Momentum**: Full demonstration of the seamless transition. Notice how the core remains engaged to prevent any loss of balance during the direction change, ensuring continuous rotational momentum from the Woman's Saida directly into the Men's Saida without a break in connection.\n- **[06:05]** - **Frame Integrity & Connection Points**: The follower emphasizes the absolute necessity of maintaining a secure connection during the change of direction. Without consistent frame resistance and tone, the rapid redirection of energy will feel disjointed or cause either partner to fall off their axis.\n- **[06:32]** - **Frame Adaptation & Posture**: Adjusting the connection based on height differences. The leader's arm placement must adapt to the follower's height to prevent breaking her structural posture. Followers must maintain an upright, natural spine alignment and avoid bending forward to compensate for a taller leader, which would compromise core stability.\n- **[07:20]** - **Spatial Awareness & Alignment**: The importance of staying \"face-to-face.\" The leader must ensure his sternum tracks the follower's center, preventing an open or misaligned frame. This direct alignment is crucial for translating torque and rotational leads effectively through the connection points.\n- **[08:01]** - **Connection Points & Body Mechanics**: Adapting connection for different body types (e.g., larger abdomens or pregnancy). The principle is to establish connection at the foremost point of contact (the belly) rather than forcing an unnatural chest connection. Forcing a high connection when body mechanics dictate otherwise will inevitably compromise the lower back and destroy the shared axis.\n- **[09:22]** - **Weight Transfer & Isolation**: Introducing touch steps into the Woman's Saida. After the first two counts, the weight is held entirely on the standing leg. The touch steps require precise foot articulation and deep core isolation, ensuring absolutely no weight is committed during the taps.\n- **[10:08]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in Touch Steps**: Demonstration of the touch steps within the pattern. The leader must clearly differentiate between a weight transfer (lead) and a touch (no weight change) through subtle variations in frame compression and release, dictating the exact amount of energy the follower should expend.\n- **[11:05]** - **Axis Shifts & Momentum Control**: Modifying the Men's Saida with consecutive balance moves. This requires significant core stability to repeatedly shift momentum backward and forward without losing the established frame or disconnecting from the partner's center of gravity.\n- **[12:08]** - **Continuous Flow & Complex Sequencing**: Full demonstration of the \"Complicated Mixt Saida.\" The sequence demands hyper-awareness of weight distribution from both partners. Every touch step and balance move must be executed with a grounded lower body and an active, communicative upper body frame to guide the partner through the rapid, consecutive direction changes.\n- **[16:25]** - **Spatial Awareness & Floor Craft**: Final notes on adaptability on the social floor. The instructors emphasize that the backward balance steps can be used strategically for floor craft. If the line of dance is suddenly blocked, the leader can utilize these steps to absorb momentum and avoid collisions while maintaining the structural integrity of the dance pattern.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/05 - Pivot slow  normal version.mp4": "- **[00:20]** - **Introduction to Rotational Mechanics**: The instructors introduce the pivot, contextualizing it within the framework of a women's saida. The objective is to master the axis of rotation and the necessary weight transfers to execute multiple pivots fluidly.\n- **[01:06]** - **Differentiating Pivot and Ocho Rotations**: A clear distinction is made between the pivot and the ocho. In the pivot, the follower is positioned on the outside foot, rotating around a shared axis. The ocho, conversely, involves a more complex, isolated rotation on the inside foot, requiring a different manipulation of torque and counterbalance.\n- **[02:00]** - **Initiating the Pivot Sequence**: The sequence begins with an extended women's saida, pausing on the fourth count. The leader must establish a solid frame and clear intention to halt the follower's forward momentum, creating the necessary compression to prepare for the rotation.\n- **[02:30]** - **Axis and Foot Articulation During Rotation**: A critical biomechanical point is emphasized: both partners must maintain their weight entirely on a single standing leg during the rotation. The leader pivots on the right foot, the follower on the left. The free foot remains unweighted, acting as a rudder to assist balance while the core initiates the rotational torque.\n- **[02:52]** - **Linear Displacement Post-Rotation**: Following the 180-degree pivot, the couple transitions back into linear movement. The leader steps backward while the follower steps forward. This requires a seamless shift from rotational mechanics back into a sagittal plane progression, maintaining frame integrity to ensure the follower moves as a cohesive unit with the leader.\n- **[03:40]** - **Reviewing the Biomechanics of the Pivot**: The instructors reiterate the importance of staying grounded on the standing leg. Weight transfer must be completely resolved before initiating the pivot; any residual weight on the secondary foot will disrupt the axis and compromise stability.\n- **[04:06]** - **Spatial Awareness and Directional Logic**: To simplify the cognitive load, the instructor provides a spatial rule: if the leader's momentum takes them backward, the first step post-rotation is with the back foot. If moving forward, the first step is with the front foot. This ensures continuous, uninterrupted flow without breaking the connection.\n- **[05:50]** - **Resolving the Sequence**: The sequence is concluded by exiting into a standard women's saida. This requires the leader to re-establish the initial frame structure and lead the follower back into the established slot, dissipating the rotational energy smoothly.\n- **[06:50]** - **Partner Connection in Closed Hold**: The demonstration in closed hold highlights the necessary tension and compression. The leader must maintain an active, slightly resistant frame to guide the follower through the rotations without using excessive arm strength, relying instead on core rotation and weight shifts.\n- **[07:55]** - **Core Isolation and the \"Opposite Saida\" Position**: The concept of the \"opposite saida\" position is introduced, focusing entirely on upper body isolation. The leader's chest is rotated to the left, contrasting with the standard rightward orientation. This requires significant thoracic mobility and core engagement to maintain frame integrity without distorting the lower body's alignment.\n- **[08:46]** - **Dissociation During the Pivot**: A vital technique is explained: during the lower body rotation, the upper body (chest) must remain fixed relative to the partner. This dissociation—rotating the hips and legs while maintaining the chest angle—creates dynamic tension and allows for a sharper, more controlled pivot.\n- **[10:14]** - **Visualizing Frame Integrity**: To aid in understanding the chest isolation, the instructors suggest a practice hold where the leader grips the follower's elbows. This tactile and visual feedback mechanism forces both partners to become acutely aware of their thoracic rotation and ensures the frame does not collapse during the lower body pivot.\n- **[11:00]** - **Couples Practice - Slow Tempo Execution**: The slow practice emphasizes maintaining the axis and connection. Leaders must focus on the precise timing of weight transfers and the initiation of torque through the core, ensuring the follower is not pulled off balance during the pivots.\n- **[13:10]** - **Musicality and Slow Execution**: Practicing to music at a slow tempo requires immense control over momentum. The couple must utilize the full duration of the beat for weight transfer, demonstrating a grounded, continuous movement quality rather than rushing through the steps.\n- **[15:33]** - **Transitioning to Normal Speed Mechanics**: The mechanics shift slightly when executing the pivot at normal speed. Instead of pausing on individual counts, the rotation occurs fluidly *between* the beats. This requires a highly responsive frame and instantaneous weight resolution, as the torque must be generated and absorbed much faster.\n- **[16:48]** - **Continuous Flow and Spatial Management**: At normal speed, the focus shifts to spatial awareness and continuous momentum. The leader must anticipate the line of dance and manage the rotational energy efficiently, ensuring the couple moves seamlessly from linear to rotational and back to linear mechanics.\n- **[18:32]** - **Final Notes on Connection and Response**: The instructor concludes by emphasizing the importance of practicing the connection. The follower must remain highly receptive to the leader's chest orientation, allowing their body to follow the lead's core initiation rather than anticipating the footwork. This relies on constant, subtle communication through the frame.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/06 - Normal  Infinite Square.mp4": "- **[00:13]** - **Introduction to the Square**: The instructors introduce the \"Normal and Infinite Square\" in Kizomba. The core mechanic relies heavily on alternating side-to-side positions, utilizing precise chest rotations to guide the follower without disrupting the line of dance.\n- **[00:31]** - **Core Engagement & Isolation**: Solo practice demonstrating the essential upper body isolation. The movement requires maximizing shoulder and chest rotation while keeping the hips strictly stabilized, ensuring the lower body does not inadvertently follow the upper body's torque.\n- **[01:14]** - **Weight Transfer & Leader Initiation**: The sequence begins with the first two counts of a Men's Saida. The leader shifts weight to step out and forward, opening the slot and establishing the initial trajectory for the follower.\n- **[01:42]** - **Spatial Awareness & Follower Mechanics**: On count 3, the follower executes a controlled side step to cross the leader's line. This step must be compact, avoiding a wide split, to maintain balance and allow for a seamless weight transfer onto the corresponding axis.\n- **[02:07]** - **Leader Collection & Axis Alignment**: Simultaneous to the follower's side step, the leader collects their left foot next to the right. Both partners must ensure their feet and body alignment point in the same direction, establishing a shared axis for the subsequent backward/forward steps.\n- **[02:35]** - **Frame Integrity & Rotational Dynamics**: Following the linear steps, the leader initiates a counter-rotation of the chest. This torque is transferred through the frame, guiding the follower to pivot and return to a squared, face-to-face closed position.\n- **[03:45]** - **Foot Articulation & Grounding**: A detailed breakdown of the critical count 3. The leader must prioritize a clean collection of the feet, while the follower must commit to a precise side step without rotating prematurely, ensuring maximum stability before the next weight shift.\n- **[04:36]** - **Leader Mechanics & Torque**: The leading mechanic for the follower's side step is entirely dependent on the leader's chest rotation from right to left. This internal torque translates into a clear, linear directional cue for the follower, moving them across the slot.\n- **[04:53]** - **Frame Integrity & Position Maintenance**: As the leader steps backward on count 4, they must actively hold their chest rotation to the left. This sustained frame tension prevents the follower from collapsing inward and maintains them securely on the leader's left side.\n- **[05:22]** - **Core Engagement & Sequential Leading**: Summary of the leader's chest dynamics: right, right, left, left, then relax. This sequential engagement of the core dictates the entire flow of the movement, highlighting how internal body mechanics drive partner connection.\n- **[06:56]** - **Frame Integrity vs. Arm Leading**: A crucial correction highlighting the danger of leading with the arms or elbows. Relying on arm strength breaks the frame's integrity, causes the follower to over-rotate or step forward incorrectly, and destroys the shared center of gravity.\n- **[07:50]** - **Biomechanics of the Side Step Lead**: Proper execution requires the leader to rotate their chest, which moves the follower from the right side to the left side in a straight line. The frame acts merely as a transmitter for the core's rotational energy, not the source of the lead.\n- **[09:09]** - **Spatial Awareness & Distance Management**: If the leader lacks the rotational range to facilitate the follower's crossing, they must increase the physical distance between partners. This adaptation requires a precise adjustment in spatial awareness to maintain connection without crowding.\n- **[10:28]** - **Tension, Compression, & Resistance**: A masterclass on frame dynamics relative to distance. When distance increases, both partners must proportionally increase tension and resistance in the frame. This active tone compensates for the lack of body contact, ensuring the lead remains clear and immediate.\n- **[11:03]** - **Dynamic Frame Calibration**: The frame must be treated as a dynamic structure. Closer proximity allows for a more relaxed, breathable connection, whereas greater distance demands a firmer, more resistant frame to transmit kinetic energy efficiently across the gap.\n- **[12:40]** - **Transitioning to the Infinite Square**: Introduction of the continuous loop. To make the square \"infinite,\" the leader modifies the exit strategy, transitioning the rotational energy back into the starting position of a Men's Saida rather than resolving to a face-to-face close.\n- **[13:28]** - **Weight Transfer & Continuous Flow**: The mechanics of the infinite loop require repeating steps 2, 3, and 4 seamlessly. The weight transfers must be continuous, requiring impeccable grounding and soft knees to absorb and redirect momentum without stalling.\n- **[14:18]** - **Resolving the Loop & Spatial Awareness**: To exit the infinite square, the leader simply alters the final chest rotation, resolving the torque to bring the follower back into the original face-to-face slot, neutralizing the rotational energy and concluding the pattern.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/07 - Follower turn meet her halfway and The Walk.mp4": "- **[00:40]** - **Initiation & Leader/Follower Mechanics**: The movement begins with a standard woman's Saida for the first three counts. The leader must establish a clear lead through the chest and frame, initiating the follower's forward and lateral trajectory while maintaining a grounded axis.\n- **[01:12]** - **Frame Integrity & Signal Application**: On count three, the leader elevates the left arm to signal an impending rotation. This elevation must be initiated from the shoulder joint while maintaining core stability, avoiding any destabilization of the leader's own vertical axis. The height of the arm must be calibrated to the follower's stature to prevent hyper-extension or breaking of her posture.\n- **[02:04]** - **Weight Transfer & Compression Generation**: Crucially, the leader steps backward on counts two and three. This posterior weight shift creates a necessary counterbalance and compression within the frame, storing kinetic energy that will subsequently launch the follower into her rotational path.\n- **[02:22]** - **Rotational Torque & Spatial Management**: As the rotation commences, the leader utilizes the left hand to provide a consistent, centripetal pull, guiding the follower in a circular orbit. Simultaneously, the leader's right hand slides across the follower's back, providing tactile feedback and defining the spatial boundary of her rotation.\n- **[03:10]** - **Tension Maintenance & Follower Autonomy**: The leader maintains a subtle, continuous tension with the left hand to sustain the follower's momentum without forcing her steps. This allows the follower spatial autonomy to express her walk, provided she maintains frame integrity and responsiveness to the established centripetal force.\n- **[04:20]** - **Trajectory Interruption & Re-connection**: To conclude the rotation, the leader halts the orbital guidance and presents an open frame to intercept the follower's path. The leader's right arm extends to welcome her back into a closed hold, requiring precise spatial awareness to align their axes smoothly on the subsequent marking step.\n- **[05:48]** - **Leader Frame Availability & Tactile Re-engagement**: During the follower's orbital phase, the leader must actively manage the right arm, keeping it relaxed but positionally ready to slide back into a supportive hold. A rigid or dropped arm will result in a clunky, disconnected re-entry, disrupting the fluidity of the weight transfer.\n- **[06:19]** - **Follower Frame Readiness & Joint Articulation**: While rotating, the follower must maintain a functional, half-bent articulation of her left arm, positioned around waist height. This constant availability ensures the leader can seamlessly re-establish connection without having to search for or force the arm into position, preserving the shared center of gravity.\n- **[08:10]** - **Axis Modification: Meet Her Halfway**: This variation requires the leader to break a static axis and initiate a leftward rotation to intercept the follower earlier in her orbit. This reduces the follower's travel distance and requires the leader to actively manage their own rotational momentum to ensure a balanced, synchronized reunion.\n- **[14:26]** - **Open Frame Position & Core Stability**: Transitioning to an open frame requires the leader to anchor the elbows against the ribcage, keeping forearms parallel to the floor. This biomechanical alignment utilizes the lats and core for stability, preventing the arms from disconnecting from the body's center and ensuring a robust, side-by-side connection.\n- **[15:21]** - **Lateral Weight Transfer & Parallel Tracking**: In the open frame walk, both partners execute lateral side steps before transitioning into a forward walk while maintaining the open connection. This demands synchronized weight transfers and strict adherence to parallel tracking lines to prevent collapsing the shared space or breaking the established tension.\n- **[18:11]** - **Responsive Connection & Isometric Tone**: The fundamental principle emphasized is that the follower must maintain isometric tone within the connection, moving only when the leader's frame displaces space. If the leader moves, the follower moves; if the leader stops, the follower stops. This requires a highly tuned tactile sensitivity and a continuously engaged core to translate the leader's subtle shifts into immediate action.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/08 - Shadow In  Out Woman  Saida Shadow version.mp4": "- **[00:48]** - **Frame Integrity & Leader/Follower Mechanics**: The instructor demonstrates the 'Shadow In' transition from a closed to an open, side-by-side orientation. Observe how the leader initiates the transition not by pulling, but by creating a clear spatial pathway and raising the left hand, maintaining a stable axis while the follower executes a half-turn.\n- **[01:06]** - **Weight Transfer & Spatial Awareness**: Breakdown of the follower's footwork for the rotation. The follower must articulate through the feet, taking controlled forward steps on the right, then left, executing the half-turn on a stable axis before stepping side to collect, ensuring precise spatial alignment relative to the leader's slot.\n- **[01:42]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Weight Transfer**: The leader introduces a syncopated footwork pattern (side, collect, side) to align their weight transfer cadence with the follower's continuous movement. This requires a quick, grounded weight shift to ensure the leader is prepared to catch the follower at the exact moment the rotational momentum is arrested.\n- **[03:47]** - **Frame Integrity & Spatial Awareness**: During the rotation, the leader's raised left hand must pass between the partnership at forehead height. This specific spatial pathway prevents distorting the follower's cervical spine and maintains the structural integrity of the rotational axis, ensuring a smooth pivot.\n- **[04:28]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Connection Points**: As the follower rotates, the leader's right hand slides across the follower's back. The leader then uses the base of the wrist against the follower's ribcage to establish compression, effectively arresting the rotational momentum through core engagement rather than relying on a gripping action.\n- **[04:48]** - **Connection Points & Hand Articulation**: Detail on the hand change at the completion of the turn. The leader lowers the left hand to drop the follower's arm, releasing the right hand from the hip to smoothly catch the follower's left hand, maintaining continuous tactile communication and frame tone throughout the transition.\n- **[05:37]** - **Frame Integrity & Core Engagement**: The instructor details the follower's free arm placement. The follower establishes an active connection by placing their hand on the leader's core, utilizing palm-to-palm connection for support. This active frame assists in maintaining balance and core stability in the offset shadow position.\n- **[06:24]** - **Connection Points & Spatial Awareness**: The leader's right-hand placement in the shadow position is refined. To prevent discomfort and maintain a respectful, functional connection, the leader uses the base of the wrist against the follower's side/hip bone rather than placing fingers directly on the stomach, adjusting the compression point based on the follower's response.\n- **[07:46]** - **Frame Integrity & Rotational Mechanics**: Biomechanics of the leader's right arm during the initial rotation from closed hold. The leader transitions from a full grip to an open \"hook\" with the arm, allowing the follower's back to slide freely while maintaining a structural safety net. This ensures the follower's rotational axis remains undisturbed if balance is momentarily lost.\n- **[08:38]** - **Spatial Awareness & Axis Alignment**: The instructor emphasizes the precise spatial relationship required in the shadow position. The leader must remain slightly offset to the side, aligning their right hip bone with the follower's left hip, rather than standing directly behind. This offset axis is crucial to avoid stepping on the follower during subsequent forward weight transfers.\n- **[09:23]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Rotational Forces**: The transition out of the shadow position (Shadow Out). The leader initiates a rotational force, guiding the follower to turn in front of them. The leader must manage the tension in the connection to ensure the follower's axis shifts smoothly from the offset side back to the front of the leader's primary slot.\n- **[11:53]** - **Frame Integrity & Core Engagement**: Troubleshooting the 'Shadow Out' rotation. The leader must maintain their left elbow within the follower's visual field. Dropping the elbow behind the follower's shoulder line compromises the frame's integrity, breaking the active connection and destabilizing the rotational axis mid-turn.\n- **[13:16]** - **Weight Transfer & Spatial Awareness**: Introduction of the Woman Saida variation from the shadow position. The follower must execute progressive steps, stepping diagonally out and then back into the slot. This requires precise spatial awareness and grounded weight transfers, particularly on the backward diagonal step, to maintain trajectory without relying heavily on the leader's frame for stability.\n- **[13:40]** - **Weight Transfer & Core Stability**: The instructor emphasizes the importance of natural foot articulation for the follower during the Saida variation. The follower must avoid anticipating the leader's steps and focus on grounding properly depending on the directional shift, ensuring core stability is maintained independent of the partner connection.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/09 - Ocho slow  normal.mp4": "- **[00:30]** - **Weight Transfer & Axis Shifts**: Introduction of the pivot mechanics central to the Ocho. The instructor emphasizes the difference between rotating on the outer versus the inner foot, highlighting how the axis of rotation shifts depending on the foot placement relative to the partner's position.\n- **[00:53]** - **Spatial Awareness & Foot Positioning**: Clarification of the \"outer\" and \"inner\" foot concepts within the context of the Woman's Saida. Understanding this spatial relationship is crucial for determining the correct axis for the subsequent pivot.\n- **[01:40]** - **Integration of Mechanics in Closed Hold**: A full demonstration of the Ocho sequence. Observe the fluid weight transfers, maintaining a grounded connection while executing the rotational forces, and the continuous spatial awareness required to navigate the line of dance post-rotation.\n- **[02:08]** - **Weight Transfer in Woman's Saida**: Breakdown of the foundational steps leading into the Ocho. The leader shifts weight backward onto the left foot while the follower transfers weight forward onto the right, establishing the necessary compression and axis for the upcoming pivot.\n- **[02:30]** - **Follower Mechanics & Core Engagement**: Detailed breakdown of the follower's pivot. The follower must completely collect her weight over the supporting right foot, engaging the core to maintain a vertical axis while executing a precise half-turn rotation initiated by the leader.\n- **[04:06]** - **Foot Articulation & Frame Integrity**: During the pivot, the follower's free foot must connect ankle-to-ankle with the supporting foot. This tight foot articulation ensures the lower body frame remains compact, allowing the rotational torque generated by the leader to transfer cleanly through the follower's core.\n- **[04:21]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Tension**: The follower is instructed to hold her structural frame and wait for the rotational lead rather than anticipating. This relies on maintaining consistent tension in the connection, ensuring the rotation is a direct result of the leader's chest and frame isolation.\n- **[04:53]** - **Grounding & Axis Stability**: A critical technical point regarding the supporting foot during the pivot. Both partners must maintain a flat-footed connection to the floor rather than rising onto the toes, ensuring maximum grounding and preventing any vertical disruption to the shared axis.\n- **[05:27]** - **Weight Transfer Post-Rotation**: Exiting the pivot, the leader utilizes a heel lead but immediately transfers the weight to the ball of the foot. This specific foot articulation allows the heel to remain in contact with the floor without bearing the full load, facilitating a smooth, grounded walking transition.\n- **[06:44]** - **Core Isolation & Leader Mechanics**: The leader initiates the follower's rotation through a precise isolation of the chest while the lower body remains stationary. This torque is transferred through a solid upper body frame, compelling the follower to pivot without the leader needing to step.\n- **[07:18]** - **Frame Integrity & Rotational Torque**: To facilitate the follower's rotation, the leader opens the frame, bringing both arms parallel. This structural shift, combined with the chest isolation, creates the necessary space and rotational torque for the follower to pivot seamlessly.\n- **[07:46]** - **Center of Gravity & Spatial Awareness**: Before initiating the turn in closed hold, the leader must manage the follower's center of gravity. By subtly bringing her weight forward, the leader ensures she is perfectly balanced over her supporting axis before applying the rotational force.\n- **[07:55]** - **Compression & Frame Resistance**: In the open frame position, the leader anchors the elbows against the chest. The follower must actively apply weight and resistance against the leader's right arm, creating the necessary compression to stabilize her rotation.\n- **[09:16]** - **Core Engagement & Structural Tone**: Emphasizing the necessity of a strong, active frame during continuous Ochos. Both partners must maintain core engagement and structural tone to effectively transmit and receive the rapid changes in rotational direction.\n- **[10:04]** - **Leader's Grounding & Vertical Isolation**: During continuous Ochos, the leader's footwork must remain completely flat and grounded. Any vertical bouncing or rising in the shoulders will disrupt the connection and destabilize the follower's rotational axis.\n- **[12:40]** - **Compensating Mechanics & Frame Tension**: Advanced frame management where the leader uses tension to compensate if the follower loses balance during the pivot. By increasing frame resistance and managing the distance, the leader can physically support and realign the follower's axis.\n- **[13:13]** - **Timing & Rotational Velocity**: Transitioning to the \"normal\" (fast) Ocho. The rotation must occur swiftly between the beats rather than spanning a full count. This requires a sharp, decisive initiation from the leader and an immediate, tight response from the follower's core.\n- **[14:00]** - **Leader Mechanics for Accelerated Rotation**: To lead the faster rotation, the leader must execute a more explosive opening of the frame and a sharper chest isolation. This increased velocity must still be controlled entirely through the core and frame, not by pulling with the arms.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/10 - Side Lean  Rhythm variations.mp4": "- **[01:14]** - **Spatial Awareness & Axis Shifts**: The instructor introduces a modification to the Woman's Saida base. On the third count, the leader executes a lateral step instead of moving backward. This deliberate spatial adjustment alters the line of dance and prepares the follower's axis for the upcoming lateral displacement.\n- **[01:50]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Frame Integrity**: Establishing the connection for the side lean on count three. The leader must maintain a solid frame with controlled compression to halt the follower's forward momentum. The follower meets this resistance, allowing her axis to be manipulated without breaking structural integrity.\n- **[02:22]** - **Follower's Weight Transfer**: Analysis of the follower's footwork in response to the leader's side step. Because she does not receive a full step indication, she must collect her feet rather than stepping through. Her weight transfers onto the supporting foot (the right foot, closest to the leader), establishing a secure base for the lean.\n- **[03:00]** - **Core Engagement & Base Stability for the Leader**: For any acrobatic or leaning move, the leader's foundation is paramount. The feet are planted shoulder-width apart, ensuring the center of gravity is perfectly balanced between both legs. The core is engaged and the spine remains straight, relying on leg strength rather than lumbar flexion to support the partner.\n- **[03:36]** - **Follower's Core Engagement During Un-balancing**: As the leader shifts the follower's axis off-center, her core must activate immediately. She must maintain a rigid, plank-like alignment from her shoulders to her heels. Any relaxation in the abdominal wall will result in a collapse of posture, compromising both aesthetics and safety.\n- **[04:12]** - **Frame Tension & Safety Mechanics**: The critical role of equal and opposite tension in the frame. If the leader applies tension to initiate the lean, the follower must match it, creating a secure structural loop. The leader's arm acts as a crucial \"safety hook\"; a relaxed frame from either partner disrupts this kinetic chain, risking a fall.\n- **[05:10]** - **Weight Transfer & Biomechanics of the Lunge**: Executing the descent for the side lean. The leader initiates a controlled lunge by articulating the left knee while keeping the torso strictly vertical. The descent and subsequent ascent are powered entirely by the quadriceps and glutes, ensuring the lower back is protected and the axis remains stable.\n- **[06:00]** - **Partner Communication During Descent and Ascent**: The precise timing required to synchronize the lean. The leader controls the velocity of the descent over two counts and the ascent over two counts through firm frame connection. The follower maintains her core isolation, moving as a single, unified block in response to the leader's muscular initiation.\n- **[08:50]** - **Follower's Foot Articulation & Styling Options**: During the lean, the follower has options for leg styling. She can maintain a straight-leg alignment or articulate the free leg by tracing the ankle up the supporting leg. This requires acute spatial awareness and core control to execute styling without destabilizing the shared axis.\n- **[11:07]** - **Weight Transfer in Slow Motion**: Transitioning to rhythm variations, starting with \"Slow Motion.\" This technique requires a hyper-controlled, continuous transfer of weight across multiple counts. The dancer must articulate through the foot slowly, maintaining constant muscle tension to avoid abrupt stops or loss of balance.\n- **[12:12]** - **Critical Timing for Deceleration**: The exact moment to initiate slow motion. Deceleration must occur precisely between the foot striking the floor and the full transfer of weight. Attempting to slow down after the axis has fully shifted over the supporting leg will lead to an immediate loss of balance and structural collapse.\n- **[13:30]** - **Break Step vs. Slow Motion Mechanics**: Differentiating the \"Break Step\" from slow motion. The Break Step demands an absolute, sudden cessation of all kinetic energy—both the feet and the upper body must freeze simultaneously. This requires extreme core isolation and instantaneous braking force from the lower body to halt momentum completely.\n- **[15:05]** - **Weight Transfer in Syncopation**: Introducing syncopated steps, requiring rapid, precise weight changes on the beat and half-beat. To maintain balance at higher speeds, the stride length must be drastically reduced. Smaller steps ensure the center of gravity remains tightly controlled over the base of support.\n- **[16:05]** - **Communicating Acceleration through Frame Integrity**: When a leader initiates a syncopated rhythm, the frame must instantly become more rigid. The rapid change in velocity is transmitted through a strong lat and core connection, rather than arm movement. This instantaneous increase in frame tone clearly signals the acceleration to the follower.\n- **[17:40]** - **Follower's Response to Rapid Rhythm Changes**: When encountering unexpected acceleration or syncopation, the follower's primary responsibility is frame integrity. She must close her eyes to anticipation and rely entirely on tactile feedback. By maintaining a locked core and active connection, she moves precisely as directed by the leader's shifting torque and compression.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/11 - Saida  Friends.mp4": "- **[00:44]** - **Weight Transfer & Frame Integrity: The Side Touch Step**: Initiate a side step, followed by a touch without weight transfer. Crucially, you must establish a rigid, tense frame to physically block your follower from transferring her weight completely. Engage your core immediately to absorb the follower's momentum and create a clear physical boundary.\n- **[01:13]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics: Managing Momentum**: During the side touch, your frame as a leader dictates the follower's weight distribution; fail to create a strong block, and she will naturally transfer her full weight. Followers, you must remain highly responsive to the compression in the leader's right arm, arresting your movement exactly when the resistance peaks.\n- **[02:02]** - **Frame Integrity: Asymmetrical Blocking Dynamics**: Blocking on the right side utilizes your left arm, which requires a delicate balance of firmness without squeezing the follower's back. Utilize your entire structural frame, driving the stop from the lats and core rather than relying solely on arm strength, ensuring a comfortable yet definitive halt.\n- **[02:50]** - **Spatial Awareness: Sequencing Consecutive Saidas**: The transition into two consecutive Woman's Saidas requires you to omit the traditional 'marca' (closing step). This demands precise spatial management; continuously redirect the follower's line of dance without breaking the flow. Keep your connection points fluid but controlled to guide the follower through the extended rotational path.\n- **[03:31]** - **Axis Shifts & Spatial Awareness: Quarter Turn Integration**: Modifying the first Woman's Saida to include a quarter turn fundamentally alters the spatial trajectory. Initiate this by stepping forward and slightly to your left, shifting the rotational axis. Followers, maintain strict frame integrity here to interpret the subtle directional change without over-rotating.\n- **[05:06]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics: Transitioning to Men's Saida**: Linking the second Woman's Saida directly into a Men's Saida involves another omission of the marca, creating a continuous kinetic chain. Seamlessly shift from guiding the follower's forward momentum to initiating your own external path. This rapid role reversal requires exceptional core stability from both partners to prevent axis collapse.\n- **[06:48]** - **Core Engagement & Spatial Redirection: Modifying the Men's Saida Exit**: Instead of stepping back in front of the follower, execute a side step on count three to bring the follower face-to-face. This maneuver relies heavily on your core engagement to draw your lower body back into alignment while simultaneously rotating the follower. Ensure your frame provides clear rotational torque without pulling the follower off balance.\n- **[08:22]** - **Musicality & Grounding: Initiating Movement**: When the music begins, it is imperative not to rush the initial steps. Focus on grounding your weight and establishing a shared axis before moving. Use soft knees and a slight side-to-side weight shift to synchronize your internal timing and frame tension with the musical phrasing.\n- **[09:48]** - **Weight Transfer: The Committed Touch Step**: Introducing the true weight transfer touch step drastically changes the biomechanics. You must fully commit your center of mass over the stepping foot (leader's left, follower's right). This requires a complete axis shift, demanding strong ankle articulation and core stabilization to prevent falling into the step.\n- **[10:39]** - **Core Engagement: Postural Integrity During Weight Shifts**: During the committed weight transfer, avoid bending the knees excessively or collapsing the torso. Keep your core actively engaged to maintain vertical alignment of the spine over the new base of support. This ensures that the return step is driven by a deliberate push from the grounded foot rather than a structural rebound.\n- **[11:43]** - **Spatial Awareness & Torque: The Pivot Execution**: The final sequence introduces a pivot, with the leader driving forward and the follower traveling backward. On the third step, generate rotational torque through your core and frame, not just your arms. Followers, maintain a rigid frame and strong core to translate this rotational force into a clean pivot around your vertical axis.\n- **[13:30]** - **Continuous Flow & Frame Integrity: The Complete Sequence**: Executing the entire routine without intermediate 'marcas' tests your ability to maintain continuous kinetic energy. Frame integrity is paramount here; any slack will result in lost momentum or miscommunication. Anticipate the next spatial trajectory well in advance, utilizing subtle shifts in compression and tension to guide the follower fluidly through the complex path.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/12 - Playing with the directions.mp4": "- **[00:25]** - **Weight Transfer & Axis Shift**: The leader initiates a forward balance step, requiring a clear shift of the center of mass over the front foot without committing full weight, then recovering backward. This establishes the foundational forward-backward axis control and tests the couple's ability to move synchronously without breaking their shared center.\n- **[00:33]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Foot Articulation**: Transitioning to a backward balance step. The leader must clearly indicate the directional change through the frame, stepping back with the right foot while the follower mirrors. The collection of feet emphasizes control over the base of support and prepares the body for the next weight transfer.\n- **[01:09]** - **Spatial Awareness & Walking Steps**: The sequence moves into alternating walk steps (forward, side). This requires maintaining a consistent distance between partners while navigating the line of dance. The leader must guide the follower's weight transfers clearly through the frame to ensure synchronous, grounded movement.\n- **[01:15]** - **Weight Transfer & The 'Marca'**: Introduction of the 'marca' (mark) step. The leader collects and restarts with the same foot (right foot for followers, left for leaders). This requires a subtle weight suspension and deliberate non-transfer of full weight to allow for the foot change, testing core stability and balance.\n- **[01:50]** - **Frame Integrity & Connection Points**: The instructor emphasizes the importance of connection for directional changes. The follower relies entirely on the leader's frame tone and resistance to interpret the new direction. A relaxed yet actively engaged frame is crucial; tension will block the signal, while lack of tone will delay it.\n- **[02:24]** - **Core Engagement & Breath**: A critical point on breathing and relaxation. Holding breath creates rigidity in the upper body, disrupting frame integrity and dampening the physical communication between partners. Breathing allows the body to move naturally, enhancing the subtle tension/compression mechanics required for fluid leading and following.\n- **[02:57]** - **Spatial Awareness & Quarter Turns**: The leader initiates a quarter turn on the right, converting a side step into a forward movement. This requires a precise rotation of the core axis and a clear redirection of the spatial slot. The follower must maintain frame integrity to be guided smoothly through the new trajectory.\n- **[03:29]** - **Leader Mechanics & Energy Transfer**: To execute the quarter turn, the leader must provide specific energy (compression/tension) through the frame to rotate the couple. Bending the knees slightly lowers the center of gravity, increasing grounding and making the rotational force more stable and effective.\n- **[04:08]** - **Weight Transfer & The Men's Saida**: Introduction of the Men's Saida starting with a single balance step. The leader steps forward, guiding the follower backward, opening the slot. This requires a strong core engagement from both partners to maintain their individual axes while managing the shifting shared center of mass.\n- **[04:24]** - **Rotational Mechanics & The Square**: The beginning of the 'square' movement. The leader collects feet and rotates the chest, leading the follower to step around the leader's vertical axis. This relies heavily on torque generated from the core and transmitted through an unbroken frame, ensuring the follower stays on the intended circular path.\n- **[04:34]** - **Spatial Awareness & Rewinding**: After the initial steps of the square, the leader reverses the direction, stepping back and 'rewinding' the movement. This demands precise spatial awareness to retrace the steps and clear communication through the frame to halt the follower's forward momentum and smoothly reverse it.\n- **[05:40]** - **Axis Shifts & Transitioning to the Side**: After the balance step, the leader moves directly into the middle of the square, then transitions to a side step. This requires rapid, controlled shifts of the vertical axis and clear weight transfers to guide the follower seamlessly from a forward/backward plane into a lateral one.\n- **[06:27]** - **Core Stability & Practice Strategy**: The instructor suggests inserting balance steps (back and forth) between elements to practice and maintain connection. This allows dancers to reset their frame integrity, stabilize their core, and recalibrate their weight distribution before initiating the next complex directional change.\n- **[07:07]** - **Integration of Mechanics to Music**: The couple demonstrates the first sequence to music. Observe how the core mechanics discussed earlier—frame tone, grounded weight transfers, and clear axis shifts—are applied fluidly in real-time, requiring constant micro-adjustments in tension and compression.\n- **[08:42]** - **Leader Mechanics & Momentum Control**: The second part begins with a modified walk where the leader stops halfway through the sequence and initiates a side step. This requires the leader to arrest the forward momentum using core engagement and frame compression, cleanly redirecting the energy laterally without pulling the follower off balance.\n- **[09:04]** - **Rotational Mechanics & Shared Axis**: The sequence introduces a half-turn. The leader uses torque from the core, transmitted through the frame, to rotate the couple 180 degrees. Maintaining a shared vertical axis and consistent connection distance during the rotation is more critical than the exact number of steps taken.\n- **[09:14]** - **Spatial Awareness & Reverse Rotation**: Following the half-turn, the leader initiates a rewind by stepping to the side and rotating right, sending the follower backward. This complex maneuver requires a deep understanding of spatial orientation and precise manipulation of tension/compression to reverse the rotational direction smoothly.\n- **[10:11]** - **Frame Integrity & Open Transitions**: The final sequence alters the exit from the walk, transitioning directly from an open frame position into a side step. This demands a rapid shift from an open, expansive connection to a lateral movement, severely testing the responsiveness and structural integrity of the follower's frame.\n- **[11:00]** - **Axis Control & Rotational Leverage**: During the transition, the leader steps backward with the right foot and turns the chest to the right. This shifts the leader's axis backward while simultaneously applying rotational force to the follower, setting up the exact spatial leverage needed for the Women's Saida entry.\n- **[11:24]** - **Elasticity in Frame Transitions**: A detailed breakdown of transitioning from open to closed frame. The leader steps back to open the frame, rotates the chest, and steps back into a normal closed hold. This requires elasticity in the arm connection and strict core stability to manage the changing distance without losing the partner's center.\n- **[12:30]** - **Full Routine Biomechanics Application**: The couple dances the complete sequence to music. Note the continuous, grounded flow achieved by maintaining soft knees. The transitions between different directional planes and frame holds are seamless due to consistent core engagement and highly responsive leader-follower mechanics.\n- **[14:09]** - **Muscle Memory & Execution Emphasis**: The instructor emphasizes practicing the transitions slowly and deliberately. This is crucial for developing muscle memory for the correct biomechanics—specifically, the subtle weight shifts, frame adjustments, and core engagements required for fluid, effortless directional changes.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/13 - Double Saida  stuff.mp4": "- **[00:13]** - **Spatial Awareness & Alignment**: The sequence initiates by aligning the follower to travel in the same direction as the leader. This requires a precise shift in spatial orientation, utilizing the foundational walking mechanics to establish a shared line of dance while maintaining connection.\n- **[00:26]** - **Frame Integrity for Directional Change**: To transition into the open position, the leader opens the frame while keeping elbows securely connected to the chest. This maintains core engagement and structural resistance, ensuring the follower feels the rotational lead through the body's center rather than the arms.\n- **[00:43]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Weight Transfer**: The leader initiates a turn to open the frame, followed by three forward walking steps. The weight transfer must be continuous and grounded. This transitions into a backward balance movement, requiring controlled axis shifts before returning to a closed, face-to-face position.\n- **[01:35]** - **Connection Points in Open Hold**: The leader releases their left hand and the follower's right hand. The free hands are held at belly level. This positioning ensures that when returning to closed hold, the hands naturally reconnect without needing to search or break upper body posture.\n- **[02:21]** - **Frame Integrity & Leverage**: In the open position, the connection relies solely on the leader's right arm on the follower's back. The leader must fully lace their arm around the follower's back to create leverage. Placing the hand merely in the middle of the back only allows for pushing, whereas full engagement is required to bring the follower back into the closed position.\n- **[03:01]** - **Spatial Management in Double Saida**: The routine incorporates a double women's saida. The leader must manage the line of dance and spatial distance twice, ensuring the follower completes the first rotation and is immediately prepped for the second without losing forward momentum or grounding.\n- **[03:26]** - **Leading the Oitso**: An Oitso (figure eight) is added on the second saida. The leader initiates this rotation using their chest and frame, causing the follower to pivot on their inner foot. The leader's core must remain fully engaged to provide a clear, stable center for the follower to rotate around.\n- **[04:46]** - **Altering the Axis Shift**: Instead of concluding the Oitso with a standard side step, the sequence changes to a forward step. This unexpected weight transfer alters the momentum and requires acute follower response to the leader's core direction and frame compression.\n- **[05:13]** - **Weight Transfer in Balance Moves**: Following the forward step, balance moves are introduced. The leader steps forward on the right while the follower steps backward on the left. The weight must shift cleanly (walk, balance, walk) while maintaining a shared center of gravity and soft knees for shock absorption.\n- **[06:26]** - **Touch Steps & Frame Tension**: Touch steps require highly precise weight management, where the foot articulates the floor without committing full body weight. The leader must utilize strong frame tension to clearly communicate these micro-shifts, preventing the follower from fully transferring their axis.\n- **[07:18]** - **Rhythmic Play & Weight Shifts**: The speed of the balance moves is altered for musicality. This requires rapid, controlled weight transfers and highly responsive frame mechanics. Both partners must keep their weight over the balls of their feet to execute the quick directional changes without losing balance.\n- **[09:32]** - **Women's Saida with Touch Step**: The women's saida is modified to start with a touch step. This requires the leader to maintain a tight, engaged frame, applying the exact same compression used in basic touch steps to clearly signal the delayed weight transfer before leading the follower out.\n- **[10:02]** - **Rotational Mechanics & Spatial Clearance**: When stepping back after the touch step, the leader must actively rotate their chest to the right. This creates the necessary spatial clearance and rotational torque to lead the follower backward. Precise timing is required to avoid stepping on the partner's feet.\n- **[10:32]** - **Foot Articulation & Grounding**: When executing the backward steps, dancers must lift their feet naturally rather than sliding them heavily on the floor. Proper articulation ensures clear weight placement and prevents drag, which can disrupt the timing and axis stability.\n- **[11:39]** - **Core Isolation & Hip Mechanics**: The final segment introduces hip movements (tarraxinha elements). The action starts with a step and a knee bend, followed by an outward hip isolation. The core must remain entirely stable to support the lower body mobility, ensuring the hips articulate cleanly on the beat.\n- **[12:22]** - **Partner Communication in Isolations**: When executing the hip isolations together, the mechanics rely on complementary movement. As one partner's hip rotates, the other's must open to accommodate the motion. The leader maintains a calm upper body, using subtle core compression to synchronize the hip articulation without disrupting the frame.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/14 - Quick Steps and Double Lean.mp4": "- **[00:24]** - **Spatial Awareness & Frame Integrity (Quick Step Intro)**: Introduction to the Quick Step, requiring lateral movement in a continuous direction. The leader must maintain a rigid frame to clearly transmit the intention of continuous side steps, preventing the follower from defaulting to a basic return step.\n- **[00:50]** - **Weight Transfer & Tension**: For the Quick Step to function, precise weight transfer is paramount. The leader must utilize increased tension in the connection to guarantee the follower receives the signal to continue laterally rather than returning to a neutral, centered balance.\n- **[01:08]** - **Core Engagement & Axis Shifts (50/50 Collection)**: When collecting the feet, the weight must briefly settle at a 50/50 distribution. To initiate the next step, the leader deliberately shifts their axis onto the standing leg, freeing the moving leg and mirroring this weight transfer into the follower's body through core connectivity.\n- **[01:50]** - **Weight Transfer (Balance Move)**: Introduction of the balance move, emphasizing a clear rocking motion forward and backward. The weight must fully commit to the stepping foot while maintaining vertical structural alignment, preparing for the upcoming rotational mechanics.\n- **[02:04]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Rotation**: Adding a counter-clockwise rotation to the balance move. The leader initiates torque from the core, applying slight rotational pressure through the frame on each weight transfer to guide the follower through a quarter-turn increment.\n- **[02:30]** - **Spatial Awareness (Foot Placement)**: A crucial note on maintaining space between the feet during the rotational balance move. Bringing the feet together neutralizes the axis; keeping them staggered allows for continuous leverage and base stability during the rotation.\n- **[03:45]** - **Frame Integrity & Leverage**: The space between the feet provides the necessary base of support to lead the follower through the full rotation. Without this widened stance, the leader lacks the grounding required to generate rotational force through the frame without pulling the follower off balance.\n- **[04:05]** - **Putting It Together (Quick Step to Rotation)**: Transitioning from the linear Quick Step into the rotational \"Clock\" movement. The core must seamlessly shift from lateral stabilization to generating rotational torque, using the collected, neutral foot position as the transition point.\n- **[04:36]** - **Weight Transfer (Syncopated Walk)**: Introducing a \"Walk, Side, Marca\" sequence with a syncopated \"1 and 2, 3 and 4\" rhythm. This requires rapid articulation of the feet and precise control of the center of mass to ensure the weight transfers completely on the half-beats without losing grounding.\n- **[05:28]** - **Spatial Awareness (Modified Saida)**: Modifying the forward turn by stepping backward to open the slot for the follower. The leader then steps to the outside track, matching the follower's line of dance to move parallel in the exact same direction.\n- **[06:05]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics (Parallel Walk)**: Executing the parallel walk requires clear, unidirectional intention. The leader must project their center of mass forward, driving through the standing leg, to encourage the follower to continue moving linearly rather than prematurely turning to face the leader.\n- **[07:05]** - **Weight Transfer & Rotation (Left Side Syncopation)**: On the left side, replacing the marca with a full weight transfer, followed by a syncopated \"side, collect, and\" while actively rotating counter-clockwise. This demands immense core stability to manage the rapid weight shifts while simultaneously handling rotational momentum.\n- **[08:35]** - **Core Engagement & Musicality**: Practicing the sequence to music. Notice how the core must remain continuously active to absorb the bounce of the beat while maintaining the structural integrity required to execute the rapid syncopations and sharp direction changes.\n- **[09:40]** - **Core Engagement & Compression (Double Lean)**: Introduction of the Double Lean. The leader initiates a sudden downward compression on count 3, instantly bending the knee. This requires immense core strength from both partners to support the sudden drop in elevation while maintaining a secure, supportive frame connection.\n- **[10:19]** - **Weight Transfer (Sequential Leans)**: Executing two consecutive leans (counts 3, 4 and 5, 6). The dancers must smoothly absorb the downward force and utilize ground reaction force to rebound, instantly transferring that upward energy back into the second compression without breaking posture.\n- **[10:48]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Rotational Force**: On the ascent of the second lean (count 6), the leader pushes powerfully off the grounded leg to launch the couple into a rapid clockwise rotation. This converts vertical compression directly into explosive rotational momentum.\n- **[12:12]** - **Core Engagement & Deceleration (The Stop)**: The critical braking mechanism for the rapid rotation. The leader must tense the leg and thigh, executing a firm marca on the floor while aggressively engaging the core to absorb the rotational inertia and instantly halt the couple's movement.\n- **[12:48]** - **Frame Integrity & Flow (Full Sequence)**: Combining the Woman's Saida, side steps, Double Lean, and rotational exit. The frame must dynamically adjust tension levels—firm for the side steps, deeply supportive for the leans, and rigid for the stopping marca—ensuring seamless, safe communication throughout the complex sequence.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/15 - Shadow Walk  Stuff.mp4": "- **[00:15]** - **Core Engagement & Dissociation**: Introduction of the foundational concept for the sequence. The movement requires strict dissociation between the upper and lower body. The core must be highly engaged to stabilize the pelvis while the thoracic spine rotates independently, allowing the leader to direct the follower without compromising their own axis.\n- **[00:30]** - **Leader Mechanics & Frame Integrity**: Execution of the Men's Saida into the first square. The leader rotates the chest to create a spatial invitation for the follower. Crucially, the chest remains fixed toward the partner while the hips rotate. This torque is managed through a stable frame, translating the rotational energy directly into the follower's pathway.\n- **[01:10]** - **Weight Transfer & Axis Shifts**: Transitioning into the second square. The mechanics alternate between chest-driven and hip-driven movements. When the hips rotate to change direction (a 1/4 turn), the weight transfer must be clean and fully committed to the new standing leg before the upper body initiates the next lead.\n- **[02:20]** - **Spatial Awareness & Lower Body Mechanics**: A critical correction on leg trajectory. The lower body must track strictly along straight lines (forward, backward, or lateral) relative to the newly established direction, rather than drifting diagonally. This maintains the structural integrity of the dance slot and ensures clear weight transfers.\n- **[03:20]** - **Frame Integrity & Hip Rotation in Ochos**: Applying the straight-line concept to Ochos. The legs maintain a linear track while the hips execute an extra rotation to strictly face the new direction of travel. This prevents the partner from being pulled off their axis and keeps the rotational energy localized in the core.\n- **[04:20]** - **Spatial Awareness & Transition to Shadow**: The entry into shadow position. The leader raises the left arm while maintaining forward progression, guiding the follower into a parallel alignment. Distance management is vital here; the leader must step slightly offset to accommodate the follower without crowding her space.\n- **[04:50]** - **Weight Transfer & Level Changes**: Walking in the shadow position with dynamic level changes. The sequence shifts from neutral walking to a pattern of stepping down into a plie, then up. The leader dictates this verticality through their own standing leg mechanics, requiring the follower to match the compression and expansion in real-time.\n- **[06:10]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Open Hold Connection**: Modifying the exit from shadow position. The leader utilizes a subtle pull to guide the follower into an open hold. The leader's elbow is anchored against their chest to establish a rigid connection point, ensuring the tensile force is transmitted through the back and lats rather than just the arm.\n- **[07:10]** - **Weight Transfer & Non-Verbal Communication**: Introduction of the knee lift. As the leader steps back, they establish a deep connection along the hip and lateral side. By freeing the inside foot and lifting the knee, the leader transfers this vertical intention through the established hip connection, prompting a mirrored response from the follower without needing to hook feet.\n- **[08:30]** - **Core Stability & Controlled Axis**: Executing consecutive knee lifts. The leader demonstrates control over the movement by lifting the knee twice before stepping forward. This requires immense core stability and a perfectly stacked axis over the standing leg, preventing any lateral sway that could confuse the follower.\n- **[08:48]** - **Frame Integrity & Re-establishing Closed Hold**: Bringing the follower back from the open position. The leader reconstructs the frame, re-engaging the chest connection. This transition must be seamless, utilizing the compression built during the open position to smoothly redirect the follower into a Women's Saida and finally a standard marca.\n- **[10:05]** - **Core Engagement & Rapid Directional Changes**: Adding advanced chest isolations and syncopations in the open position. The leader rapidly brings the follower in and out, demanding high core reactivity from both partners. The syncopated footwork (pam-pam-pam) replaces the standard weight transfers, requiring crisp, articulate steps to stay on timing.\n- **[11:20]** - **Sequence Integration & Spatial Management**: Full walkthrough of the complete sequence. Notice how the transitions between squares, shadow position, level changes, and open holds rely on constant management of the line of dance. The leader continuously recalibrates their frame and weight distribution to maintain a fluid, unbroken connection throughout the complex spatial changes.\n- **[13:30]** - **Musicality & Dynamic Application**: Dancing the sequence to music. Observe how the theoretical biomechanics translate into fluid movement. The grounding through the floor dictates the smoothness of the level changes, while the active frame ensures that every syncopation and isolation is sharply communicated to the follower perfectly on the beat.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/16 - Back and Forth.mp4": "- **[00:32]** - **Weight Transfer & Axis Shift in \"Salsa\" Step**: The leader initiates a forward weight transfer on the left foot, immediately shifting laterally to the right, and collecting. This requires precise core engagement to maintain an upright axis without pitching forward. The follower mirrors this, managing backward momentum to prevent pulling on the leader's frame.\n- **[01:11]** - **Syncopated Weight Transfer**: Introducing syncopation (\"1 and 2\") alters the duration of ground contact. The feet must articulate faster, requiring the core to fire more rapidly to stabilize the upper body. The weight transfer becomes sharper, demanding an active frame from both partners to transmit the timing change clearly without muscle tension.\n- **[02:11]** - **Frame Integrity During Touch Steps**: In the modified Woman Saida, the touch step relies heavily on frame integrity rather than weight transfer. The leader uses the frame to halt the follower's momentum, converting kinetic energy into a suspension. Both partners must maintain a toned core to prevent collapsing the connection point during the \"touch.\"\n- **[03:22]** - **Spatial Awareness & Core Engagement in Men Saida**: The leader alters the traditional Men Saida by maintaining chest orientation toward a specific wall rather than opening the chest out. This requires significant lower body rotation and hip isolation, powered by core engagement, allowing the leader to cross the slot while keeping the connection point stable for the follower.\n- **[04:08]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics in Pivots**: Adding continuous pivots requires shared axis control. The leader initiates rotational torque through the core and lats, communicating the turn through a stable frame. The follower responds by keeping their center connected to the leader's, using grounding through the metatarsals to execute the pivot smoothly without drifting out of the slot.\n- **[04:31]** - **Suspension and Unweighted Foot Articulation**: The stop on the touch step is a masterclass in weight control. The body's momentum is arrested completely on the supporting leg, requiring deep core stability and a soft knee. The tapping foot is entirely unweighted, serving as a stylistic accent rather than a structural support.\n- **[04:57]** - **Syncopated Rotational Torque**: Syncopating the Men Saida with pivots demands rapid adjustments in rotational force. The leader must apply and release torque quickly, requiring a highly responsive frame from the follower. The compression between the partners must be maintained to ensure the syncopated timing translates perfectly into rotational movement.\n- **[06:46]** - **Grounding and Reversal Mechanics**: Reversing direction using touch steps requires shifting from linear momentum to a static hold, then reversing. The weight must be fully committed to the supporting leg to free the tapping foot. The core acts as the braking mechanism, and the leader uses slight compression in the frame to communicate the change in direction to the follower.\n- **[08:26]** - **Transitioning Slots and Frame Resistance**: Exiting the reversed Men Saida into the Woman Saida involves crossing slots. The leader must clear the path while providing a clear physical boundary (resistance) with the frame to guide the follower. The follower uses this resistance to find their axis and step forward confidently into the new spatial alignment.\n- **[11:12]** - **Distance Management in \"Back & Forth\"**: The \"Back & Forth\" relies entirely on the elasticity of the connection. The leader initiates backward momentum, and the follower matches it. Frame integrity is crucial here; if the arms extend too much, the connection breaks. The core must remain engaged to keep the distance consistent between partners as they shift weight.\n- **[12:38]** - **Counterbalance and Leader Initiation**: For the styling on count 5, the leader releases the closed hold and initiates a stylized push. This relies on momentary counterbalance. The leader must lower their center of gravity slightly to ground the push, while the follower accepts the energy, using their core to absorb the momentum and prepare for the return.\n- **[13:01]** - **Tension/Compression in Open Styling**: The \"hand throw\" is an illusion; the control comes from the palm connection. The leader applies clear compression, signaling the follower to suspend. The follower must maintain tension in their arm and back muscles (lats) to prevent the shoulder from disengaging, utilizing the elasticity to rebound back to the leader.\n- **[15:08]** - **Lower Body Isolation and Grounding**: The final side steps incorporate a knee bend and lateral hip movement. This is a pure lower body isolation. The upper body frame must remain perfectly horizontal and quiet. Deep grounding into the floor is required to allow the hips to articulate sideways without disrupting the partner connection.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/17 - Ocho  Stuff.mp4": "- **[00:24]** - **Weight Transfer & On-Site Walking**: Initiation of the basic on-site walk, emphasizing alternating weight shifts. The core remains engaged to maintain balance over the standing leg, ensuring a clear axis before the next step. This foundational movement requires precise articulation through the feet to facilitate smooth, grounded transitions.\n- **[01:12]** - **Lateral Weight Shift & Collection**: Execution of a touch step to the side followed by a collection. The leader must clearly transfer their center of mass laterally, guiding the follower to match the displacement. The collection phase demands strict core stability to pause the momentum without losing the active frame connection.\n- **[02:20]** - **Frame Integrity during Shadow Rotation**: Transitioning into a side step shadow position. The leader uses a syncopated step to initiate rotation, requiring a firm frame to transmit the rotational torque. The follower must maintain a responsive core and maintain the established spatial distance to execute the half-turns smoothly.\n- **[02:54]** - **Leader Mechanics & Spatial Awareness**: Lowering the hand serves as a clear physical cue to arrest the follower's rotation. This manipulation of the connection point communicates a boundary within the slot, re-establishing a closed or semi-closed spatial relationship.\n- **[03:25]** - **Safety Hook & Frame Tone**: The leader employs a \"safety hook\" with the right hand to secure the follower's balance during rotation. This technique relies on adjusting the frame's tension and compression dynamically, ensuring the follower remains centered on their axis while navigating the rotational momentum.\n- **[03:44]** - **Sliding Connection & Isolation**: The leader's left hand slides along the follower's shoulder during the rotation. This requires upper body isolation to maintain the connection without restricting the follower's turn. The core must stay stable to anchor the movement while the arm articulates through the changing spatial geometry.\n- **[04:25]** - **Exiting Rotation & Frame Release**: The leader initiates the exit by directing the elbow outward, unlacing from the follower's shoulder. This action demands precise spatial awareness to clear the follower's line of dance, shifting the compression to guide them back into a neutral, closed position.\n- **[04:54]** - **Double Ocho & Axis Shifts**: Introduction of the double ocho, initiated from a Woman's Saida. The leader must create clear, distinct rotations of their chest to guide the follower's pivoting axis. Each ocho requires a complete weight transfer and a momentary stabilization before the next rotational force is applied.\n- **[05:20]** - **Tension, Compression, & Balance**: Emphasizing the use of the partner's frame for balance during the slow ocho rotation. Both dancers must engage their lats and core, creating a mutually supportive structure of tension and compression. This shared center of gravity is crucial for executing controlled, balanced pivots.\n- **[05:40]** - **Chest Rotation & Forward Lean**: The leader leans slightly forward and rotates the chest to the left to initiate the follower's pivot. This postural adjustment changes the angle of compression, clearly signaling the direction of the ocho. The core acts as the primary driver for this thoracic rotation.\n- **[06:10]** - **Dynamic Frame Adjustments for Ochos**: The leader dynamically opens and closes the frame on alternating sides (right open, left close) to lead the consecutive ochos. This requires a highly responsive frame and clear spatial management to dictate the follower's rotational degree without collapsing the connection points.\n- **[06:47]** - **The \"Sexy Spoon\" & Core Engagement**: A conceptual visualization for maintaining a rounded, engaged frame during the ochos. The arms and chest create a continuous loop of energy, requiring sustained core activation. This prevents the frame from becoming rigid or disconnected during the rotational and linear shifts.\n- **[07:13]** - **Follower Mechanics: Collection & Patience**: The follower is instructed to collect the free foot against the standing leg and wait for the leader's rotational cue. This demands acute sensitivity to weight transfer and strict axis control, ensuring they do not preemptively pivot or fall off their center.\n- **[08:00]** - **Mirroring Weight Transfers in Reverse Ocho**: The leader steps backwards, mirroring the follower's forward steps during the reverse ocho. This requires precise spatial awareness to maintain a consistent distance between partners. The weight shifts must be perfectly synchronized to keep the frame compression constant.\n- **[08:45]** - **Spatial Management & Troubleshooting**: If the pattern becomes messy or space is limited, the leader is advised to execute the steps on-site. This highlights the importance of spatial adaptability, using core control to absorb momentum and maintain the pattern's integrity within a confined slot.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/18 - Hips move and Sit.mp4": "- **[00:17]** - **Weight Transfer & Axis Shift**: The foundational \"walk touch\" sequence relies on precise weight transfer. The leader initiates a forward weight shift, committing fully to the walking step, followed by a light tap that requires no weight transfer, maintaining the axis over the standing leg. This creates a grounded, continuous pulse without fully stopping momentum.\n- **[00:30]** - **Frame Integrity & Connection**: The leader is instructed to maintain a supportive frame without applying downward pressure on the follower. By maintaining a stable, neutral connection, the leader relies on core engagement rather than arm strength to guide the follower's vertical \"down and up\" motion. Compression is held steadily to communicate the rhythmic change.\n- **[00:43]** - **Core Engagement & Verticality**: The biomechanics of the \"down\" motion require softening the knees while keeping the spine aligned. The descent happens simultaneously with the full weight transfer of the walking step. The subsequent \"up\" motion on the tap aligns the body back to a neutral, extended axis, requiring core stability to avoid leaning forward or backward.\n- **[01:14]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Spatial Awareness**: During the transition from the Men's Saida into the Women's Saida, the leader must actively manage the slot. On the third step of the Men's Saida, the leader steps back onto the line of dance, immediately opening the spatial path for the follower to initiate her Saida, utilizing body rotation to invite her forward.\n- **[02:12]** - **Timing Alteration & Frame Resistance**: The standard Women's Saida is modified by delaying the follower's exit from the slot. The leader maintains a squared frame and connection through the second step, blocking the follower's lateral trajectory. It isn't until the third step that the leader introduces rotational torque to guide her out, requiring an active response from the follower's core to redirect her momentum.\n- **[03:14]** - **Spatial Awareness & Trajectory Management**: Entering the open square (quadrado) variation, the leader uses a side step to displace off the line of dance, simultaneously guiding the follower backward. This requires acute spatial awareness to establish a new parallel slot. The leader then steps into the space between them, establishing an inner track to lead the subsequent rotational figures.\n- **[04:24]** - **Leader Mechanics & Dynamic Compression**: To execute the speed change (acceleration), the leader must generate an impulse from the core, not just the arms. The leader drops their center of gravity slightly to increase grounding, utilizing a sharper contraction of the lats to instantly communicate the required velocity change to the follower.\n- **[05:31]** - **Weight Transfer & High-Velocity Footwork**: The leader must increase the cadence of their own weight transfers to physically drive the acceleration. This involves quicker foot articulation—striking the floor with precision and immediately collecting the center over the new standing leg. The chest must actively lead the rotation, dictating the increased speed of the follower's pivots.\n- **[06:44]** - **Spatial Reorientation & Open Hold Transition**: Exiting the Saida, the leader guides the follower into a perpendicular alignment, opening the slot to a side-by-side orientation. The frame transitions from a closed embrace to an open connection, requiring both partners to activate their lats to maintain horizontal tension and compression without the support of full body contact.\n- **[07:29]** - **Core Isolation & Grounding**: The \"Hips Move\" introduces lateral and vertical isolation. Both partners must maintain a soft knee bend to establish deep grounding. The core is engaged to isolate the pelvic movement from the ribcage, allowing the hips to shift laterally while the upper body maintains structural integrity and frame connection.\n- **[08:14]** - **Leader/Follower Mechanics & Tactile Feedback**: The magnitude of the hip movement is negotiated through the frame. The leader initiates a subtle lateral weight shift and reads the follower's responsiveness. If the follower yields smoothly, the leader can increase the amplitude of the isolation, communicating strictly through the connection points at the shoulder and lower back.\n- **[10:49]** - **Momentum Control & Slow Motion**: Executing the slow-motion transition requires intense core stability and controlled eccentric muscle contractions. The leader must decelerate the rotational momentum through a sustained, continuous weight transfer, guiding the follower to do the same. This prevents an abrupt stop, transforming kinetic energy into suspended, controlled movement.\n- **[12:35]** - **Spatial Awareness & Axis Alignment for the Sit**: Positioning for the \"Sit\" requires the leader to place their right leg precisely under the follower's center of gravity. The leader creates a physical shelf by maintaining a strong, externally rotated thigh. Spatial alignment is critical; the leader's axis must remain upright to counterbalance the follower's downward and backward trajectory.\n- **[13:10]** - **Biomechanics of the Half Squat & Core Support**: The leader executes a unilateral half-squat on the left leg to lower the level, keeping the chest lifted and the core heavily braced to protect the lumbar spine. Simultaneously, the right arm provides a supportive frame around the follower's back. The right leg acts as a structural pillar, absorbing the follower's weight without collapsing inward.\n- **[13:38]** - **Follower Mechanics & Self-Suspension**: The follower must engage their core aggressively to avoid becoming dead weight on the leader. The sit is not a complete release of muscle tone; it is a controlled suspension. The follower maintains an active connection through the leader's frame, utilizing their own abdominal and back muscles to support their upper body weight while resting lightly on the leader's leg.\n- **[14:51]** - **Foot Articulation & Base of Support**: The leader's foot placement is essential for stability during the sit. The leader steps forward with the left foot, establishing a wide, grounded base of support, then brings the right leg across the slot to intercept the follower's path. The weight must remain centered between the feet to handle the dynamic load shift.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/19 - Running footwork.mp4": "- **[00:48]** - **Weight Transfer & Syncopation Mechanics**: Notice the syncopated weight shift used to alter the expected rhythm. By executing a step-collect-step sequence, you catch up to the follower's natural two-step walk. This demonstrates precise control over your axis shifts and rhythmic interpretation without disrupting your partner's flow.\n- **[01:56]** - **Core Engagement in Men's Saida**: The initiation of the men's saida relies on a grounded side-to-side weight transfer. Keep your core active to stabilize your upper body frame. This ensures the lateral movement is communicated clearly to the follower through the connection points rather than relying on arm movement.\n- **[02:11]** - **Frame Integrity & Rotational Torque in Reverse Ochos**: Shift your weight to the left foot to establish a solid axis. By opening the frame and rotating the chest to the right, you generate torque from the core. Transmit this rotational force through the frame, leading the follower into the ocho while both partners manage their individual balance.\n- **[03:04]** - **Spatial Awareness & Leading the Cross**: Initiate a forward weight transfer on the left foot while managing the spatial distance. By subtly shifting the follower's axis over her right foot and guiding her trajectory, she is naturally prompted to cross her steps. Emphasize leading through body positioning and spatial management rather than forcing the movement with your hands.\n- **[03:33]** - **Grounding & Spatial Management in the \"Beach Step\"**: When transitioning to a side-by-side walking pattern, maintaining an open stance is crucial. Manage the spatial distance between your feet to provide the follower with an unobstructed path. This requires grounded foot articulation and continuous core stability to move in parallel without collision.\n- **[04:51]** - **Foot Articulation in Syncopated Walks**: The introduction of the walk-touch sequence demands sharp foot articulation and clear weight changes. Execute cross-and-cross steps while leading the follower through touch steps. This requires precise compression and tension within your frame to communicate the rhythmic variations clearly.\n- **[05:37]** - **Axis Shifts & Body Rotation**: Execute a backward walk followed by a complete body rotation to realign face-to-face. This movement requires a strong, grounded axis on your supporting leg and significant core engagement to drive the rotation. Maintain frame integrity to guide the follower securely through her corresponding side step.\n- **[07:01]** - **Frame Integrity during the Opening**: Rotate on the right foot and open the frame to transition the follower into a side-by-side position. This requires a sudden shift in tension and compression. Utilize your lats and core to absorb the rotational energy and halt the follower's momentum safely beside your axis.\n- **[07:41]** - **Leg Connection & Impulse Mechanics**: Establish physical contact using your knee and thigh against the follower's leg. By rotating your body to the right and applying a controlled push, you create a clear impulse. This requires precise spatial awareness and core stability to deliver a guided force rather than a disruptive hit, facilitating her leg lift and cross.\n- **[08:29]** - **Continuous Connection & Rotational Forces**: During the opening and leg push sequence, maintain continuous leg contact by sliding your leg along the follower's. This tactile connection, combined with an upward and forward impulse, dictates her leg trajectory. Keep your core engaged to counterbalance the rotational forces as the follower pivots around your axis.\n- **[11:40]** - **Core Isolation & Rotational Leading**: Lead the follower around your axis without shifting your own feet by utilizing core isolation. Keep your frame solid to transmit the rotational torque directly to the follower. Use tension to guide her circular path while maintaining a static, grounded base.\n- **[12:15]** - **Spatial Awareness in Open Position Walks**: When walking forward and backward in an open side-by-side position, constantly adjust your spatial alignment. Take slight lateral steps to the right during the walking pattern to ensure the slot remains clear. This spatial management keeps the connection points at optimal tension and distance.",
  "Isabelle  Felicien - Beginner/20 - Lock  Tarraxinha.mp4": "- **[00:40]** - **Rhythmic Variation & Basic Step**: Introduction of the syncopated rhythm. The focus here is on altering the timing of the standard basic step while maintaining the same spatial footprint. The core must remain engaged to control the deceleration during the slow steps and acceleration during the syncopations.\n- **[00:48]** - **Weight Transfer in Slow Motion**: Execution of the slow steps. Notice the deliberate articulation through the foot—ball to heel—ensuring a smooth, continuous weight transfer. The grounding here is crucial; the axis must remain perfectly vertical as the weight shifts from one leg to the other.\n- **[01:14]** - **Syncopated Walk Mechanics**: The quick, syncopated steps require a lower center of gravity. The knees must soften further to absorb the rapid weight changes. The frame remains quiet, ensuring the rhythmic change is communicated through the body's center rather than the arms.\n- **[01:30]** - **Axis and Weight Distribution**: Emphasis on having the weight fully committed to the standing leg. The free leg must be completely unweighted to allow for the rapid succession of steps that follow. This requires precise core control to prevent the upper body from swaying.\n- **[01:40]** - **Linear Walk Execution**: A series of quick walking steps. The line of dance is strictly maintained. The leader must drive the movement from the center, compressing slightly forward into the frame to invite the follower to travel backward without breaking posture.\n- **[03:25]** - **Leader's Mechanics in Women's Saida**: Modifying the leader's footwork during the Saida. The leader uses a balance step to change direction, redirecting the momentum. The frame must maintain consistent tone to guide the follower along her path while the leader preps for rotation.\n- **[03:57]** - **Rotational Path & Spatial Awareness**: The leader executes a half-circle walk around the follower. This requires stepping forward on the left foot and carving an arc. The frame acts as the radius of this circle; if the tone drops, the connection points will collapse, and the rotation will stall.\n- **[04:30]** - **Advanced Syncopation Options**: Introduction of more technical syncopation options. The leader marks rapid weight changes to cancel out momentum, demanding high responsiveness from the follower. The core must be tight to stabilize these abrupt stops and starts.\n- **[05:28]** - **Cross Step Biomechanics**: A detailed breakdown of the cross-step syncopation. As the foot crosses, the hips must isolate slightly to allow the legs to cross without twisting the upper body frame. The grounding remains deep, pushing into the floor to maintain balance during the cross.\n- **[06:50]** - **Planting the Partner & Frame Integrity**: The leader stops the follower's lateral movement to walk around her. The key here is an intentional downward compression through the frame—pressing into the floor to \"plant\" her axis. This creates a fixed point around which the leader can orbit.\n- **[07:25]** - **Isolating Frame During Orbit**: As the leader moves around the planted follower, the frame must remain absolutely stable relative to her. The leader's body moves, but the arms and connection points remain fixed in space. This requires immense core isolation and shoulder stability to prevent pulling the follower off her axis.\n- **[08:20]** - **Weight Shift for Rotation**: To initiate the follower's rotation, the leader shifts her weight onto her front foot. This is achieved by adjusting the frame's angle and applying slight forward compression. Once her weight is committed forward, the rotation becomes effortless and grounded.\n- **[11:30]** - **Tarrachinha Core Mechanics - Connection vs. Bumping**: The fundamental concept of Tarrachinha. The movement is about establishing connection and then releasing it, not bumping or striking the partner. The core engages to bring the centers together, and then controls the release back to a neutral posture.\n- **[11:50]** - **Wall Drill for Spatial Awareness**: Demonstrating the connection concept against a wall. The goal is to touch the wall without rebounding off it. This translates to partner work: the energy should be absorbed and controlled through the core and legs, maintaining a soft, continuous flow of tension and release.\n- **[12:35]** - **Executing Tarrachinha Isolations**: Applying the technique with the partner. The leader initiates the connection through the center, and the follower responds by matching the resistance. The frame stays quiet; the movement is entirely driven by pelvic tilts and core isolations.\n- **[14:15]** - **Vertical Level Changes**: Adding level changes (up and down) to the Tarrachinha. This requires deep flexion in the knees and ankles while keeping the spine long. The core must support the lower back to prevent arching as the hips isolate vertically.\n- **[15:43]** - **Lower Body Options for Leaders**: The leader has the option to move the free leg or use deeper hip rotations. If rotating the hips, the standing leg must be firmly grounded, using the floor as resistance to create torque. The upper body frame remains undisturbed by the lower body's complex movements.\n- **[16:40]** - **Partner Communication and Comfort**: A crucial note on spatial awareness and consent. The closeness required for Tarrachinha relies heavily on mutual comfort. The leader must constantly read the follower's frame for tension or hesitation, ensuring the connection remains respectful and fluid."
};
